zed  that for her the beast was this  beautiful wave that she wanted  everything to do with yeah so we're all  different yeah and then in the book you  can see the illustration it shows uh she  has an inhaler  yes and that's something you you've  talked about that you have asthma how  has that impacted  you and why was it so important to add  that detail yeah i grew up with asthma  and um  swimming was very important for me  actually sports was very important for  me to develop my lungs and to keep  working  on my health and  you know asthma made me have limitations  and i dealt with those limitations my  whole career and especially in the  beginning when miasma was more  was stronger than it is today so asthma  was my limitation that in the end you  know because  asthma dealt with breeding and i went  into a path that  often i'm underwater without breath  it became kind of my strength you know i  turned that around where you know i knew  what it was like to be out of breath  and so there was a comfort there that i  could translate once i went into the  waves and i was underwater and i  couldn't breed  i had that since i was born yeah i want  you to take us on the board  in the water  riding a wave like that 73 and a half  foot monster like what is going through  your mind when you are  in the middle of dominating a wave this  size ooh  um that's the beauty of it not much is  going through my mind really i'm living  in the moment and i'm like having to  adjust and adapt to everything that's  happening around me which is a lot you  know the way that the wave is shaping  the line i want to take  how i want to make a critical ride but  also be safe and end the ride and and  the channel and be picked up by my  partner so i'm  living in the moment which i find is one  of my favorite parts of big wave surfing  because it is so intense and demands so  much focus that you're in the flow  there's no way  out you know your your instincts are so  alive and you're really working with the  knowledge you have created before how do  you do this though i mean you almost  lost your life in 2013 after having an  experience like that how are you able to  still be in the moment as you put it  well the moments afterwards were  horrible  but then i accepted that  you know life is not always easy and and  life is not always you know winning and  i went through a four-year period of  spine surgeries failed spine surgeries  and traumatized and it was hard to go  back to my sport but also at the same  time  it was going to be harder to give up on  everything i had worked towards you know  i was 26 i was an established big wave  surfer and i had just found the new well  it had just been discovered the new  frontier of our sport which was nasa  report to go and to walk away from that  to me was crazy it was crazier than keep  working maya gubera thank you so much  for joining us maya and the beast goes  on sale tomorrow  we will be right back
 

